@Board editor
The board editor is editing boards. The initial mode is normal mode; some
commands change modes, as described below.

The possible modes are:
!norm;Normal mode / draw mode
!mass;Mass mode
!text;Text mode
!bloc;Block mode

Others:
!flood;Flood fills
!mark;Marks
!info;Board info
!colon;Colon commands
!stat;Stats
!resize;Resizing

:norm;Normal mode / draw mode
ESC        Exit
TAB        Toggle normal/draw mode
RETURN     Pick and edit parameter of tile at cursor
0-9        Numeric prefix
ALT+I      Board info
ALT+R      Resize board
ALT+T      Test game
^O         Stat list
^V         Toggle view mode
SPACE      Toggle mark
c          Select drawing colour
d          Delete tile at cursor
e          Edit tile
f          Flood prefix
F          Flood prefix
g          Get colour from cursor
h j k l    Move cursor
i          Invert mark
m          Mass mode
o          Toggle overlay editing
p          Place tile in main layer
q          Write tile in main layer
Q          Write tile in under layer
t          Text entry mode
u          Unmark all
v          Block mode
y          Copy tile from main layer (place with p)
Y          Copy tile from under layer
 :         Colon commands
F1-F12     Custom menus
[ ]        Go to previous/next board
{ }        Go to first/last board
< >        Go to northwest/southeast corner of board

:bloc;Block mode
Most commands in normal mode are also available.

ESC        Cancel block mode
;          Exchange corners
c          Unmark block and cancel
d          Delete in block and cancel
H          Flip horizontal and cancel
i          Toggle marks in block and cancel
m          Mark block and enter mass mode
p          Masked paste and cancel
s          Mark block and cancel
u          Unmark entire board grid and cancel
V          Flip vertical and cancel
SPACE      Toggle marks in block and cancel

:mass;Mass mode
Most commands in normal mode are also available.

Some commands (marked by ø below) can be entered in uppercase or lowercase.
All of them will affect marked tiles and then cancel the mass mode, but the
lowercase commands also unmark the affected tiles while uppercase commands
leave them marked.

Cursor movement in mass mode will also move the marks, and will copy the
marked tiles from where the mark was to the new position of the marks.

ESC        Cancel mass mode
cø         Set colour
dø         Delete
fø         Place floor
pø         Place

"Place floor" will place in the main layer if it is empty, but otherwise
will place in the under layer. Existing floors will be unaffected.

:text;Text mode
In text mode, printable ASCII characters will enter that character of text
and automatically moves the cursor right. Pushing return will go to the
next line, at the horizontal position that you entered text mode.

ESC        Cancel text mode
^P         Enter special character

:flood;Flooding
The f or F prefix is used for the flooding commands. You can optionally
add a numeric prefix (before f or F) to limit the maximum range of the
flood. After f or F you must specify the flood type, which can be:

;          Don't care
cø         Match kind and colour
kø         Match kind
pø         Match kind, colour, and parameter

Any command marked ø above can be entered in uppercase in order to require
matching both the main layer and the under layer.

The matching tiles will be marked. Regardless of the flood type, any tiles
that are already marked will stop the flood from propagating.

Also, using the f prefix will flood in four directions (not diagonally),
but F prefix will flood in eight directions.

:stat;Stats
The stat list (CTRL+O) displays a list of all of the stats; each line is
displayed in a format like:
  1  Name         (00000,00000,00000) L=00000 C=00000 S=00000

Their meanings are, in order:

* The stat number (1-based).

* The name of the stat, if it has one (i.e. the first line of that stat's
script starts with a at sign).

* The (Misc1,Misc2,Misc3) values, which have no built-in meanings and can
be used for your own use.

* L is the length of the script.

* C is the number of tiles that use this stat.

* S is the speed of the stat (the number of turns between the stat update
events for tiles belonging to that stat).

The order of execution for stats is always in their numerical order,
although some stat numbers may also have special meanings according to
the templates in use.

Stat 1 generally has a special meaning; it represents the player. Many
features in the game engine treat it in a special way.

If you push enter then you can edit that stat. Most of the settings are the
same as explained above; "Text" allows to edit the script.

"XY List" will display a list of the tiles that use this stat. In this
submenu, "Sort stat XY list" allows sorting them according to your own
criteria, and "Reverse" will reverse the order of this list; this can be
useful to change the order of execution.

You can also use "Exchange" to exchange a stat with another one of a
different number (and all tiles of those stats are automatically updated),
and "Delete" to delete this stat.

:resize;Resizing
The resize menu (accessed by ALT+R in the main board editor screen) can be
used to resize a board.

You can specify the new width and height; the smallest possible size is 1x1
and the biggest possible size is 9999x9999. Select "Use cursor position" to
automatically set the new size so that the bottom-right corner will be at
the cursor position.

You can also specify what it should do with the existing grid:

* If you select one of the four diagonal compass directions then that
corner will remain as that corner of the resized board; the rest of the
board is truncated if made smaller, or filled with empty slace if larger.

* If you select "Center" then it is the center of the current board which
will be the center of the resized board, but is otherwise like the above.

* "Tile" and "Tile with duplicate stats" will tile the newly added cells
with the existing board; their differences is how stats are affected (the
former does not add stats to the new tiles, but the latter will add new
stat XY items (with the same stat numbers) as existing tiles).

For the first five options, you may select two additional options:

* "Fill main with current" fills the main layer of the newly added space
with the captured tile (with the "y" command) instead of blank space.

* "Fill overlay with NW tile" fills the overlay of the newly added space
with a copy of the overlay tile at (0,0).

Neither fill option will copy stats.

:over;Overlay editing
The overlay editing mode allows editing the overlay, which uses the same
coordinates as the main board but is independent from it; objects can move
in the main layer without affecting or being affected by the overlay.

Most commands are like the normal mode but there are some differences.
Some of the commands which are only applicable to the main layer are not
available in overlay mode.

;          Select drawing character
T          Text entry mode; set as visible
F1-F4      Toggle user flags
F5         Toggle solid
F6         Toggle reserved
F7         Toggle bg thru
F8         Toggle visible
F9         Clear flags
F10        Clear flags; set visible flag only

:colon;Colon commands
These colon commands can be used for various batch operations.

Most of these commands allow a prefix; this can be & for marked cells only,
~& for unmarked cells only, % for all cells, . for the cell at the cursor,
or one or two coordinate pairs. If there are coordinate pairs, put : in
between. Each coordinate pair otherwise uses two numbers with a comma in
between, being the zero-based X and Y coordinates. If a number is preceded
by - or + then it is relative to the cursor.

There can be zero or more filters (with no spaces in between). A filter
preceded by ~ is inverted. They can be:

* A colour/kind/parameter in square brackets. Inside of the brackets, it
can optionally be preceded by = for main layer only or _ for under layer
only; if neither, then it checks both layers. This will match only tiles
that have the specified colour/kind/parameter. The colour and parameter are
both optional; if they are omitted then they are not checked.

* An ampersand to match only marked tiles (like the & in the prefix).

* Two numbers both with + or - in front. These are relative X and Y
coordinates, and cause further filters to operate on the new coordinates;
the command listed at the end also operates on the new coordinates if they
have not been explicitly reset at the end of the filter list. If the new
coordinates are out of range of the board, then it will not match at all.
(This can result in a syntax ambiguity. This filter is allowed to have a ~
prefix to avoid the ambiguity, although the ~ prefix has no effect.)

* A slash followed by a uppercase or lowercase letter denoting the filter
type, followed by zero or more numeric arguments of the filter. The numeric
arguments have commas in between, and can have an optional minus sign.

* /C will override the colour of the argument tile with the colour of the
current tile. (This can be used with commands such as "place", together
with offset filters, to copy colours of nearby tiles.)

* /T is like /C but overrides the entire tile and not only the colour.

* /m will select only some tiles, according to a pattern. The first two
arguments are the X and Y multiplier, the third argument is the modulus,
and the rest specify what values of remainders of the above calculation
denote tiles to be selected.

* /r will select tiles at random. The first argument is the denominator
and the second argument is the numerator of a probability. If the arguments
are omitted, then "/r2,1" is assumed.

* /s will select only tiles with stats. If an argument is specified, then
only tiles whose stat are the specified stat number are selected (zero will
select tiles where at least one layer does not have stats); otherwise it
will select tiles with any stats at all. It looks on both layers.

An optional space is allowed after the last filter and before the command.

The format of a tile specification (a colour/kind/parameter) is like that
in scripts but it is less elaborate. It is an optional colour (two hex
digits with <> around), a element kind name, and an optional parameter
(two hex digits with <> around; which is the same format as the colour).

Many colon commands have abbreviations; these are listed after the slash in
the below listings of the commands.

board/b
  Switch to a board number specified as the argument (in decimal).

color/c
  Changes the colour of main layer cells. The argument can be a hexadecimal
  colour code; if not specified then the current colour is used.

count/co
  Tells you how many cells in range match the filter.

debug
  This command is for debugging Super ZZ Zero and the meaning might be
  changed in future implementations. It is not fully documented here.

delete/d
  Delete main layer cells. (Whatever is in the under layer will be moved to
  the main layer.)

exchangelayer/xl
  Exchanges the main layer with the under layer.

export/ex
  Exports the board to the file specified as the argument; it is an error
  if the file already exists. If it starts with | then instead it is a
  operating system command to execute, and the board data is written to
  that command's standard input. (See fileformat.doc for documentation
  about the format of this file.)

hflip
  Flip the area horizontally. Marks and filters cannot be used, but you
  can specify the coordinates of a rectangular area.

import/im
  Imports the board from a file specified as the argument. If it starts
  with | then it is an operating system command to execute, and that
  command's output will be treated as the board data to be imported.

mark/m
  Marks cells.

markonly/mo
  Marks cells, but unmarks any cells not marked by this command.

overdelete/od
  Delete overlay cells.

place/p
  Place a tile. This is either the current tile or a specified tile.

status
  Toggles the status display. If the argument is 0 then it turns off the
  status display, if 1 then turns on, if omitted then toggled.

toggle/t
  Toggles marks.

unmark/u
  Unmarks cells.

vflip
  Flip the area vertically. Marks and filters cannot be used, but you can
  specify the coordinates of a rectangular area.

write/w
  

writeunder/wu
  

